+# Summary: `plan_retention`
+
+## What changed
+
+- `vaultsnap/retention.py`: implemented `plan_retention(snapshots, policy, now)`. It only plans; nothing is deleted. `Plan` and the signature are unchanged.
+- `tests/test_retention_rules.py` (new): 28 tests for the rules that `tests/test_retention.py` does not cover. No existing test was edited or deleted.
+- `SUMMARY.md`: this file.
+
+Nothing else was changed. The stale ADR index, the README retention draft and the CHANGELOG are not updated (see "Needs attention" below).
+
+## Which record governs which part
+
+Status lines were read for all ten records (0001 to 0010). The index in `docs/adr/README.md` is stale: it stops at 0007, so it was not used.
+
+- **Inputs and validation** (ADR-0001 "Inputs"): naive datetimes, including `now`, raise `ValueError`; duplicate ids raise `ValueError`.
+- **Parents** (ADR-0007): a parent that is not in the plan, and a cycle (including a self-parent), raise `ValueError` whatever the policy.
+- **Ordering** (ADR-0001 "Ordering"): newer means later `taken_at`; ties go to the greater id. Used for "newest" everywhere.
+- **Order of overrides** (ADR-0008 list, with line 4 added by ADR-0009):
+  1. protected tag: kept, set aside (ADR-0001);
+  2. `taken_at` later than `now`: kept, set aside (ADR-0001);
+  3. expired tag: pruned, excluded from every rule (ADR-0008);
+  4. in the keep-within window: kept, set aside (ADR-0006 definition, ADR-0009 placement);
+  5. everything else: "remaining", the only input to the rules.
+- **keep_within_hours** (ADR-0006): inclusive boundary, measured on real instants. `None` and `0` are off. It counts as the sixth rule, so with only this rule set, older snapshots are pruned.
+- **keep_last and bucket rules** (ADR-0001 "Rules" and "Buckets", as amended by ADR-0006 and ADR-0008): each rule keeps the newest N buckets that contain a remaining snapshot, and the newest snapshot of each. The rules are independent (union). With no rule on (ADR-0001 as amended by ADR-0006 and ADR-0008), every remaining snapshot is kept.
+- **Weekly keys** (ADR-0003, which replaces the "Weeks" section of ADR-0001): ISO (year, week), Monday start.
+- **Day boundary** (ADR-0004): `tz_offset_minutes` and `day_start_hour`; all keys come from one shifted local date.
+- **Named zones** (ADR-0005): with `zone` set, the offset comes from the table at `taken_at` and `tz_offset_minutes` is ignored. The table lookup follows the record's rule (the latest row not later than the instant, the initial offset before the first row).
+- **Monthly keys** (ADR-0010, which amends the monthly row of ADR-0001): billing cycles starting on `month_start_day`, clamped to the month length. Yearly keys stay the calendar year.
+- **Dependency step** (ADR-0007): after everything else, the parents of kept snapshots are kept, transitively. Held snapshots do not count toward any rule, and the step overrides line 3 of ADR-0008. Only kept snapshots hold parents.
+- **Output** (ADR-0001): `keep` and `prune` each hold every id once, newest first.
+
+**Not in force, and why:**
+- ADR-0002 (UTC buckets): superseded by ADR-0004. Its defaults are the same as ADR-0004's defaults (offset 0, hour 0), so the default behaviour is the same, but the record is not applied.
+- ADR-0001 "Weeks" (Sunday start): superseded by ADR-0003.
+- ADR-0006 "Interaction with the bucket rules" (window counts toward the rules): reverted by ADR-0009.
+- ADR-0004's sentence "no daylight saving handling and no zone names": superseded by ADR-0005. The rest of ADR-0004 still applies.
+- The README "Retention" section (Sunday weeks, UTC midnight, protected tags): an old draft. It was not used.
+
+## Verification
+
+- Baseline before the change: `python3 -m unittest` gave `Ran 7 tests ... FAILED (errors=7)`. Every error was the `NotImplementedError` stub.
+- After the change: `python3 -m unittest` gives `Ran 35 tests in 0.002s ... OK` (Python 3.14.3, run from the repository root, the same command as CI).
+- Expected values in the new tests were worked out by hand from the records, not copied from the output. Three of my first expectations were wrong, and I checked each one:
+  - Two test failures came from my test data: snapshots dated after `NOW` (2026-03-20) are correctly set aside as future snapshots. I gave those tests a later `now`. I did not change the code.
+  - One expectation was wrong because `keep_within_hours` counts as a rule (see above). I added `keep_last=1` to that test.
+- I removed the `__pycache__` directories my test runs created. `git status` now shows `M vaultsnap/retention.py` and `?? tests/test_retention_rules.py`, and nothing else.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- **Not run:** the CI configuration (none is in the repo), the nightly job, the `vaultsnap` CLI, and real snapshot data. Retention config is still not wired into the CLI (the CHANGELOG says so), so nothing calls `plan_retention` yet.
+- **Other Python versions:** only 3.14.3 was used. The code uses only the standard library.
+- **Zone tables:** copied from ADR-0005, which says they are the complete definition and make no claim about real-world rules.
+- **Readings I chose:**
+  - A policy with only `keep_within_hours` prunes everything older than the window. This follows from ADR-0001 and ADR-0006, but it is consequential, so operators should confirm it.
+  - Ties in `taken_at` are broken by the greater id, as ADR-0001 says. Snapshots with different offsets are compared by instant.
+  - A snapshot that is set aside (protected, future or in the window) also holds its parent, because it is kept.
+
+## Needs attention (outside the allowed files)
+
+- `docs/adr/README.md` has no rows for 0008 to 0010, and its Status column is out of date for 0001, 0004, 0006 and 0008. Those records amend other records; the index should say so.
+- `README.md`: the retention section is an old draft that contradicts the ADRs (Sunday weeks, UTC midnight). It should be replaced or removed.
+- `CHANGELOG.md`: the "Unreleased" entry "Retention pruning is being added" should become a release note once the nightly job is live.
